{"author":{"id":"673e1e1b6fccff4da5524407c8a8cdc7caf18221aabb93f2bb36ffef5c2a95e8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1781334971,"nonce":"J9Cu4CKCEkGbNejO5BLvqTQDO2s=","metadata":{"github-id":"I_kwDOSn4-Gc8AAAABFXFHmw","github-url":"https://github.com/kanya-approve/karpenter-provider-rackspace-spot/issues/22","origin":"github"},"title":"Cheapest-first fallback to next ServerClass for unfulfilled pools (reconciliation-based)","message":"Follow-up to #15. `pickInstanceType` now picks the single cheapest compatible ServerClass and `instance.Create` creates one pool (`desired=1`). If that pool is never **fulfilled** — the spot bid loses the auction, or the ServerClass is capacity-constrained — the NodeClaim is stuck until Karpenter's registration TTL recreates it (and re-scheduling will usually re-pick the same cheapest class, so it can wedge repeatedly).\n\n### Why this can't be a synchronous Create-time fallback (unlike GCP)\n- **AWS** hands EC2 Fleet up to 60 candidates and lets the fleet allocation strategy pick at launch.\n- **GCP** price-sorts the compatible types and tries them cheapest-first in a loop, falling through on a *synchronous* `tryCreateInstance` error.\n- **Rackspace**: the pool API takes a **single** ServerClass, and `instance.Create` returns as soon as the pool is created via API. Fulfillment (winning the bid / a node registering) is **asynchronous**. So there is no synchronous error at Create time to fall through on — a GCP-style loop can't observe the failure.\n\nTherefore the fallback must live in a **reconciliation loop** that observes fulfillment state over time.\n\n### Sketch\n- **New reconciler** (or extend an existing controller) keyed on Karpenter-managed pools.\n- **Signal for \"not fulfilled\":** inspect `SpotNodePool.Status` (e.g. `WonCount == 0` / losing `bidStatus`) and/or no Node registered for the NodeClaim within a grace period. Confirm the exact SDK status fields (`pkg/providers/instance` already maps `WonCount`, `Status`).\n- **Action:** roll the pool to the **next-cheapest** compatible ServerClass and update the NodeClaim's `node.kubernetes.io/instance-type` label so state stays consistent.\n- **Bounding:** cap attempts (don't cycle forever); log the attempt order and the reason each class was abandoned (no silent truncation).\n\n### Refactor needed\n`pickInstanceType` currently computes the single best. Factor the price-ranking out into a reusable \"rank compatible instance types by price for a capacity type\" helper so both the initial pick and the fallback iterate the same ordered list.\n\n### Open questions\n- Grace period before declaring a pool unfulfilled (spot auctions settle on their own cadence — confirm with Rackspace).\n- Interaction with Karpenter's own NodeClaim registration TTL / disruption — avoid double-handling (provider rolls the pool vs. Karpenter recreates the NodeClaim).\n- Does rolling the ServerClass in place play nicely with the `nodelink` providerID rewrite, or should it recreate the pool under the same NodeClaim UID?\n\n### Acceptance\nA pool unfulfilled past the grace period is replaced by the next-cheapest compatible ServerClass, bounded and logged, without leaking the prior pool.","files":null}]}